# ==========================================================================
#  O28  Minimal: as few terms as will answer the questions
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    The Trail has twenty-five classes because ten questions need them; a
#    class that no question needs is not added, and gist's ninety-six for a
#    whole enterprise is the standard to be measured against.
#
#  WHY
#    gist describes itself as 'the maximum coverage of typical business
#    ontology concepts with the fewest number of primitives and the least
#    amount of ambiguity', and it does it in about a hundred classes,
#    sixty-odd object properties and fifty datatype properties. That is an
#    enterprise. The Trail, with ten questions to answer, has twenty-five
#    classes and eighty-four properties, and the properties are the number
#    to watch: every one is a column someone will have to fill.
#
#    Every term costs the same whether it is used or not: a label in each
#    language, a definition, a review, a place in the documentation, a
#    check in the gate, and a decision every time someone wonders whether
#    to use it or its neighbour. A term nobody uses is defined badly,
#    because nobody finds out. The discipline is the one from o07: a term
#    is admitted when a question needs it, and the question is written down
#    beside it.
#
#    The same discipline applies to axioms. gist keeps domains and ranges
#    sparse and declares no inverses, not from laziness but because each
#    one is a commitment that some future dataset will break, and o31 takes
#    those two up. Minimal is not small for its own sake. It is the number
#    of terms at which each one is still looked after.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#                               classes   object props   datatype props   for
#       gist 14                    ~96          ~66             ~50       an enterprise
#       the Trail                   25           32              42       ten questions
#
#       each term costs: a label per language, a definition, a review,
#       a place in the docs, a check in the gate, and a decision every
#       time someone wonders whether to use it
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - A term is admitted by a question, not by a noun in the brief. Keep
#      the question beside the term.
#    - Properties are the number to watch. Each is a column someone
#      fills, and the Trail has nearly as many as gist has classes.
#    - Minimal means every term is still looked after. That is the size
#      to aim for.
#
#  CORNER    thought
#  DATA      01-vocabulary.ttl
#  RUNS IN   the browser
#  OPEN IN THE EDITOR
#            https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F05-principles%2Fo28-minimal-as-few-terms-as-will-answer-the-questions.ttl
#  SOURCES   gist: a minimalist upper ontology
#            https://github.com/semanticarts/gist
#            OBO Foundry principle FP-005, scope
#            https://obofoundry.org/principles/fp-005-delineated-content.html
#  MEASURED  parses, 17 triples; 2 classes; OWL 2 DL in profile; OQuaRE DIT 2 (5), ANOnto 2.17 (5); 'Every class in the shipped v' 17 rows
# ==========================================================================

@prefix bs:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dct:  <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

# Borrowed annotation properties, declared so the file is OWL 2 DL on its own.
skos:prefLabel      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:definition     a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:scopeNote      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .
dct:source          a owl:AnnotationProperty .

<https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema>
    a                   owl:Ontology ;
    skos:prefLabel      "The Bookshop Trail schema"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote  "Twenty-five classes, thirty-two object properties, forty-two datatype properties, ten annotation properties, for ten competency questions. Compare gist 14: about ninety-six classes for an enterprise."@en .

# One class and its question, side by side.
bs:TrailSegment
    a                owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel   "Trail Segment"@en ;
    skos:definition  "A walking route between two bookshops, with a length."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote   "Exists for question 3: what can be walked to from a shop, and how far."@en .

# One that was proposed and refused.
bs:Festival
    a                   owl:Class ;
    owl:deprecated      true ;
    skos:prefLabel      "Festival"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote  "Proposed because Wigtown and Hay have festivals. Refused: no competency question mentions one. Kept as a deprecated stub so the refusal is on record."@en .
owl:deprecated  a owl:AnnotationProperty .


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  AFTERWARDS.  Queries to run in the SPARQL panel, on this file (press
#  Add Prefixes first) or on the data file it names.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  -- Every class in the shipped vocabulary, with what it exists for  (on the data)
#  PREFIX owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#>
#  PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
#  SELECT ?class ?comment WHERE { ?class a owl:Class ; rdfs:comment ?comment . FILTER(STRSTARTS(STR(?class), "https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#")) } ORDER BY ?class
